WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:20.769 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I'm Nicolas and this is my wife Rebecca  and we're from winter garden which is  just outside of Orlando Florida growing  up I was never bad with money when I  went into the Marine Corps the natural  thing to do is back from a deployment is  to buy a brand new car with a car  payment because that was what people did  everybody has a car payment when I was  growing up my grandfather was kind of  the smart one with money and he would  always say when he turn 18 make sure you  get a credit card so that you can get  your credit score up and be able to get  a house and this man I was like okay I  used it I paid it off every month  probably for the first six months and  then I was like oh I have all this  credit I can use so that was kind of the  start of my being normal  Nick got back from Afghanistan and we  moved down here officially  and we shortly after bought this house  and we were just living the dream we had  this beautiful house we were newly  married we started buying a lot of  things bought new furniture we bought a  few motorcycles we of course needed new  cars at that point if we wanted to buy  something we bought it there was never  really a financial discussion having to  come across Dave Ramsey on the radio  when somebody was doing there definitely  scream and that first debt-free scream  pretty much got me hooked I wanted her  to have that same feeling as I did and  then I finally talked her Becca in to go  on a Financial Peace University and I  think that was the turning point she  talked me into going to financial peace  by saying to me we could pay off all of  our debt we could have no car payment  everything changed once we went through  that class  it took a good 3 to 6 months to finally  knock out a budget without disagreement  involved the every dollar budget at the  beginning of every month made us come to  the table together and talk about our  finances I think that right there helped  our relationship especially the  communication portion of being in a  marriage it forced us to communicate we  really hit the ground running we put all  the desks in a row it worked like he  said it made us feel good it made us  feel like we were rolling that snowball  then we got to a point where we needed  more money so Nick started selling  everything he had a garage full of toys  we had a garage sale we sold one of the  cars that we had car payments on and I  got a little 2 thousand dollar car to  commute back and forth to school but no  air conditioning or anything like that  selling them helped us knock out big  parts of our debt immediately and see  that progress and know that we were  getting somewhere and we we had a goal  in mind one of the main things that  motivated me and kept me going was  having Jackson it was so motivating to  know that I was going to leave a smart  financial legacy for my son in the end  we ended up paying off about a hundred  and thirty six thousand dollars in debt  in just about 30 months it wasn't easy  if there are a lot of sacrifices that we  had to make our communication has grown  and we've grown together in marriage as  a couple it was all worth it it was  definitely worth it to now live be able  to live our life like no one else  we since have saved up to buy our first  car in cash and I tell everybody about  it I see we got a new car we bought in  cash  growing up in living in our society it's  considered impossible almost to save up  money and go and buy something any type  of thing that we want we are now  budgeting and saving for it and just  being debt-free is just a weight off our  our lives I guess you could say so I  looked online for different debt-free  screams Becca and I wanted to go to  Nashville and do our debt-free scream  talking face-to-face with Dave it was so  fun just to go there and sign the debt  free wall and have a cookie at Martha's  place and be there with Rebecca and  Jackson had just experienced like none  other  [Music]  [Music]

